  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "Choosing The Right Tool",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. How memo Gets Defeated"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "jsx",
          "lines": [
            "<Row onPick={() => pick(id)} style={{p:8}} />",
            "// new fn + new object every render",
            "// shallow compare fails, memo does nothing"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Cheaper Than Memo"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Colocate: push state to the smallest owner",
            "Smaller re-render radius, zero API cost",
            "`children` as a prop: parent renders, subtree does not",
            "Those elements were created by the grandparent"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Two Myths"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Splitting state does not multiply renders — batching",
            "Effects that set state add a pass before paint",
            "Chained interdependent effects multiply that cost",
            "Usually it signals a modeling error"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Measure First"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "DevTools Profiler flamegraph",
            "The 'Highlight updates' setting",
            "`<Profiler>` onRender callbacks",
            "React Compiler moves memoization to automatic"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Snapshots, Not Variables",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Every Render Has A Snapshot"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Each render captures its own props, state and handlers",
            "`n` is a `const` inside that render's function body",
            "Setting state schedules a new render, never edits this one",
            "Old callbacks keep reading the old snapshot forever"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "jsx",
          "lines": [
            "const [n, setN] = useState(0);",
            "setN(n + 1);",
            "console.log(n); // logs the OLD value",
            "// n is a const captured by this render"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. Reads Are Not Synchronous"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`setCount(1)` does not assign to `count`",
            "The next render receives the new value",
            "Need it now? Keep it in a local variable",
            "Log inside an effect to see committed state"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "jsx",
          "lines": [
            "setCount(1);",
            "console.log(count); // previous value",
            "// reads hit this render's snapshot"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "8. Two Setters, One Increment"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "jsx",
          "lines": [
            "setN(n + 1);",
            "setN(n + 1);",
            "// both read the same n, net +1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Both calls read `n` from the same snapshot",
            "React queues `0 + 1` twice, result is 1",
            "Batching is not the bug, stale reads are"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "9. The Updater Form Fixes It"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "jsx",
          "lines": [
            "setN(p => p + 1);",
            "setN(p => p + 1);",
            "// each gets the latest queued, net +2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Updater receives the latest queued value, not the snapshot",
            "Use it whenever next state depends on previous",
            "Safe inside effects, timers and event handlers"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Mutation And Cleanup",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "10. State Must Stay Immutable"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Never mutate arrays or objects held in state",
            "`push`, `splice`, `sort` all edit in place",
            "React compares with `Object.is`, not deep equality"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "jsx",
          "lines": [
            "state.items.push(x);",
            "setItems(state.items);",
            "// same reference, no re-render"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "11. Object.is Sees No Change"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "jsx",
          "lines": [
            "arr.push(4); // mutates in place",
            "obj.x = 1;   // mutates in place",
            "// Object.is(prev, next) === true"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Same reference in, same reference out, no render",
            "The UI silently keeps showing stale data",
            "Hardest bug to spot: no error, no warning"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "12. Copy, Then Set"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "jsx",
          "lines": [
            "setItems([...items, x]);",
            "setObj({ ...obj, x: 1 });",
            "// new reference, React re-renders"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Spread into a fresh array or object",
            "`map` to replace one item, `filter` to remove",
            "Use `toSorted` or copy before sorting"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "13. Cleanup Is Not Optional"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "jsx",
          "lines": [
            "useEffect(() => {",
            "  const id = setInterval(tick, 1000);",
            "  return () => clearInterval(id);",
            "}, []);"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Return a cleanup function from every subscribing effect",
            "React runs it before the next effect and on unmount",
            "Intervals, listeners, sockets, observers all need it"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "14. Duplicate Handlers Compound"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Missing cleanup adds a handler on every dep change",
            "Ten renders, ten intervals, ten times the work",
            "Leaks survive unmount and fire on dead components",
            "Strict Mode double-invokes effects to expose this"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Derived State Anti-Patterns",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "15. The Redundant State Trap"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Storing computed values in state creates sync bugs.",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "If it can be derived, do not store it",
            "Single source of truth prevents drift",
            "Effects that sync state are usually wrong"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "16. The Anti-Pattern"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "jsx",
          "lines": [
            "useEffect(() => {",
            "  setFullName(first + ' ' + last);",
            "}, [first, last]);",
            "// causes a second render pass"
          ],
          "note": "Avoid this pattern entirely"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Effect runs after first render",
            "State update triggers second render",
            "UI flickers or shows stale value briefly"
          ],
          "style": "cross"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "17. The Correct Way"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "jsx",
          "lines": [
            "const fullName = first + ' ' + last;",
            "// computed during render",
            "// always in sync, zero cost"
          ],
          "note": "Preferred approach"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Compute directly in the render body",
            "No effect, no state, no sync issues",
            "React re-renders only when inputs change"
          ],
          "style": "check"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "18. When To Use useMemo"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Scenario",
            "Use useMemo?",
            "Reason"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Simple string concat",
              "No",
              "Cheap operation"
            ],
            [
              "Expensive filter/map",
              "Yes",
              "Avoids re-computation"
            ],
            [
              "Object creation",
              "Maybe",
              "If passed to memo child"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "19. Derived State Rules"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Derive during render, not in effects",
            "Only memoize if computation is expensive",
            "Trust React's reconciliation, not your cache"
          ],
          "style": "checkbox"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Key Rule",
          "text": "State is for data that changes over time, not data that can be calculated."
        }
      ],
      "subtitle": "Compute, Don't Store"
    }
  ],
  "caption": {
    "text": "Part 2 of REACT HOOKS HANDBOOK. Most React hook bugs are one bug wearing different masks: your callback is reading an old render's variables. This handbook takes 10 pages to go from what a hook actually is, to the linked list on the fiber that makes the rules of hooks non-negotiable, to the cleanup functions that decide whether your app leaks. Deps, batching, custom hooks, and the 13 gotchas that ship to production — save it for your next code review.",
